## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- D0 baseline
Carried from the state immediately before this case: the T44967 mirror had been removed and
proven gone, `show mirror` empty, and ../work-box/cap-after-rm.err records
"0 packets captured / 0 packets received by filter" with ping-after-rm.txt at 0% loss.
  PASS. The destination port is silent before this case configures anything.

### STEP 1 -- "set the mirror direction to receive" and read it back
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction receive
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive                 <-- direction as set
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.48
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
  => STEP 1 PASS: the command is accepted on the destination port naming the source, and both
     show forms report destination port1.0.10, direction receive and monitored port1.0.48.

### STEP 2 -- first measurement: NO frames copied  (this is defect M1, not a feature failure)
host# ./cap.sh rx        (../work-box/cap-rx.txt, cap-rx.err, ping-rx.txt)
  cap-rx.err:  0 packets captured / 0 packets received by filter / 0 packets dropped by kernel
  ping-rx.txt: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  Nothing was copied although `show mirror` reported the mirror Enabled and correct.

  Diagnosis before concluding anything -- "DUT not mirroring" vs "host not capturing":
x960-56YSQ#show interface port1.0.10 status
  port1.0.10  -   connected   1  a-full a-1000  10GBASE-TM     <-- link is up
host# ip -br link show dev eth3
  eth3  UP  00:0d:b9:55:77:0e <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> <-- host NIC is up
  tcpdump reported "0 packets RECEIVED BY FILTER" -- i.e. nothing reached eth3 at all, so this
  is not a filter or a decode problem.
x960-56YSQ#show interface port1.0.10      (before and after 10 pings)
  output packets 86779 ... -> output packets 86790          delta +11 in ~11 s
  That delta matches the 1-per-second loop-detect frames (`loop-protection loop-detect
  ldf-interval 1` is enabled on every port), NOT mirrored traffic. So the DUT genuinely was not
  copying frames.
  Further fresh applies all captured nothing: ../work-box/cap-tx2.txt, cap-rx2.txt, cap-tx3.txt,
  cap-rx3.txt and raw-rx.txt are each 1 byte, and raw-rx was an UNFILTERED capture -- not one
  frame of any kind arrived at eth3 in 14 s.

### STEP 3 -- bounce the destination port, then measure: receive mirrors ONLY ingress
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#shutdown
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no shutdown
host# ./cap.sh rx-bounce  (../work-box/cap-rx-bounce.txt, .err, ping-rx-bounce.txt)
  cap-rx-bounce.txt:
    09:02:23.565262 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 1, length 64
    09:02:24.070480 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 2, length 64
    09:02:24.582518 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 3, length 64
    09:02:25.094491 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 4, length 64
    09:02:25.606498 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 5, length 64
    09:02:26.118491 IP 10.38.22.65 > 10.38.22.70: ICMP echo request, id 26228, seq 6, length 64
  ping-rx-bounce.txt: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS (ingress on 1.0.48) = 6 ; echo REPLIES (egress on 1.0.48) = 0
  => STEP 3 PASS: six requests and ZERO replies -- the exact inverse of T44967's six replies and
     zero requests, on identical cabling with the identical flow. The requests are what the DUT
     RECEIVES on port1.0.48. Only the received direction was copied, which is what
     `direction receive` means. Forwarding was undisturbed. T44967 is the control for this
     result and this is the control for T44967; neither could be produced by a mirror that
     ignored direction.

## Defects -- both reproducible, both measured here

### M1 -- a RE-APPLIED mirror copies nothing until the destination port is bounced
Full evidence table from ../work-box/, in time order, ping at 0% loss throughout
(a 1-byte cap file means nothing was captured; ~540-553 B means frames arrived):

  cap file                      time   state                                        frames
  cap-d0.txt                    08:54  no mirror (baseline)                          none  correct
  cap-tx.txt                    08:55  transmit, FIRST EVER apply on this port        YES
  cap-rx.txt                    08:57  removed, receive applied fresh                 none  <-- M1
  cap-tx2/rx2/tx3/rx3.txt       08:58  further removals and fresh applies             none  <-- M1
  cap-rx-bounce.txt             09:02  receive + destination port bounced             YES
  cap-tx-fresh-nobounce.txt     09:05  removed, transmit applied fresh, no bounce     none  <-- M1
  cap-tx-fresh-bounce.txt       09:06  same, then destination bounced                 YES

The only mirror that worked without a bounce was the very first one on a port that had never
been a mirror destination. Every later application required `shutdown` / `no shutdown` on
port1.0.10 before a single frame was copied, and `show mirror` reported the mirror as Enabled
with the correct direction and source the whole time.

### M2 -- changing `direction` in place is SILENTLY IGNORED
With `direction receive` live and working:
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit
                                            <-- accepted, NO error of any kind
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive                 <-- STILL reports receive, not transmit
host# ./cap.sh tx-nobounce (../work-box/cap-tx-nobounce.txt, 553 B)
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 6 ; echo REPLIES = 0      <-- still RECEIVE behaviour
Bouncing the port did not help either (cap-tx-bounce.txt: 6 requests, 0 replies). Only
`no mirror interface port1.0.48` followed by a fresh apply changed the reported direction:
x960-56YSQ#show mirror  ->  Mirror direction: transmit
and after a bounce, cap-tx-fresh-bounce.txt held 6 echo REPLIES, 0 requests.

Why M2 matters: the running configuration is not what was typed, and `show mirror` agrees with
the STALE value, so there is no way to detect it from the CLI. A test that reads back
`show mirror` and stops there would record a false pass. This is why every verdict in this group
rests on captured frames. CR wording is the Test Engineer's call; these are the measurements.

VERDICT: PASS -- `mirror interface port1.0.48 direction receive` is accepted on the destination
port, reported correctly by `show mirror` and `show mirror interface port1.0.48`, and copies
ONLY the frames the DUT receives on the monitored port (6 requests, 0 replies), the exact
inverse of T44967's transmit result on identical cabling. Two defects were found while proving
it -- M1, a re-applied mirror needs a destination-port bounce, and M2, an in-place direction
change is silently ignored -- neither of which is part of this case's step text and neither of
which prevented `direction receive` itself from behaving correctly once applied.
